ny part.*  \n   Use the \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-k\\).  \n   The points that are still uncovered form a right‑angled triangular lattice\n   \\[\n   T_k=\\{(x,y)\\mid x\\ge 1,\\;y\\ge n-k+1,\\;x+y\\le n+1\\},\n   \\]\n   which contains exactly \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\) points.\n\n   *Step 4.2 – sunny part.*  \n   For each \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) define the line  \n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;y = \\frac{i-(n-k+1)}{\\,k+1-i\\,}\\,x + \\frac{(n-k+1)(k+1)-i(k+1)}{\\,k+1-i\\,}.\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   The two points\n   \\[\n   P_i=(i,\\;n-k+1),\\qquad Q_i=(k+1,\\;i)\n   \\]\n   belong to \\(T_k\\); by construction \\(L_i\\) is the line through \\(P_i\\) and \\(Q_i\\).  \n   A direct computation shows that for every \\(i\\)\n\n   * the slope of \\(L_i\\) is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\); hence \\(L_i\\) is sunny;\n   * the sets \\(\\{P_i,Q_i\\}\\) for different \\(i\\) are disjoint, and every point of \\(T_k\\) appears in exactly one of the pairs \\(\\{P_i,Q_i\\}\\).\n\n   Consequently the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) cover all points of \\(T_k\\) and no two of them coincide.  \n   Together with the \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines we obtain a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny ones.\n\n5. **Impossibility of \\(k=2\\).**  \n   Assume a configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists. Then at least \\(n-2\\) lines are non‑sunny.  \n   Because there are only three families of non‑sunny lines, by the pigeon‑hole principle at least one of the families is used at most \\(\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{3}\\rfloor\\) times. In particular, there is a row, a column or a diagonal that is **not** represented among the non‑sunny lines.  \n\n   Without loss of generality suppose the row \\(y=n\\) is missing.  \n   The three points\n   \\[\n   A=(1,n),\\qquad B=(1,n-1),\\qquad C=(2,n-1)\n   \\]\n   all belong to the required set (they satisfy \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)).  \n   Any line that contains two of them is respectively vertical (\\(A