diate values \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\).*  \n   Write \\(k=n-t\\) with \\(t\\ge 1\\).  \n   \u2013 **Step\u202f1 (non\u2011sunny part).**  Take the \\(t\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   V_j:\\;x=j\\qquad(j=1,\\dots ,t).\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   These are non\u2011sunny and together cover all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) whose first coordinate does not exceed \\(t\\); i.e. they cover  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal C_t:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\mid a\\le t\\}.\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   \u2013 **Step\u202f2 (sunny part).**  \n   For each remaining column \\(a\\in\\{t+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) choose a single point of that column, namely \\((a,1)\\).  \n   For the \\(k=n-t\\) columns we construct \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines, each passing through the chosen point of a different column.  \n   Concretely, for \\(r=1,\\dots ,k\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   a_r:=t+r,\\qquad\n   m_r:=r\\;( \\text{any non\u2011forbidden, pairwise different slopes}),\n   \\]\n\n   and define  \n\n   \\[\n   S_r:\\; y-m_r x = 1-m_r a_r .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(S_r\\) contains the point \\((a_r,1)\\) and, because its slope is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\), it is sunny.  Distinctness of the slopes guarantees that the \\(S_r\\) are pairwise distinct.\n\n   \u2013 **Step\u202f3 (verification of covering).**  \n   Every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) belongs either to \\(\\mathcal C_t\\) (hence is on one of the vertical lines (2)) or has first coordinate \\(a\\ge t+1\\).  In the latter case the point lies in column \\(a\\).  The vertical lines do not meet this column, but the sunny line \\(S_r\\) with \\(a_r=a\\) meets the column at the lattice point \\((a,1)\\).  All other points of the same column have larger second coordinate; they are not on \\(S_r\\) (otherwise the line would have slope \\(-1\\)).  Nevertheless they are already covered by the vertical lines because the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(b\\le n+1-a\\le n+1-t\\le t\\) when \\(a\\ge t+1\\).  Hence \\(b\\le t\\) and t