y covered by the vertical lines because the condition $a+b\\le n+1$ forces $b\\le n+1-a\\le n+1-t\\le t$.  Hence $b\\le t$ and the point $(a,b)$ belongs to $\\mathcal C_t$, contradicting the assumption that it was not in $\\mathcal C_t$.  Consequently **every** point of $\\mathcal P_n$ is on at least one of the $n$ lines constructed above.*”\n\n**Verification of Step 1:**  Correct.  The $t$ vertical lines $x=1,\\dots ,x=t$ cover precisely the set $\\mathcal C_t$ of points whose first coordinate does not exceed $t$.\n\n**Verification of Step 2:**  The definition of the sunny lines $S_r$ is mathematically sound: each $S_r$ passes through $(a_r,1)$ and has slope $m_r=r$, which is neither $0$, $\\infty$, nor $-1$.  The $k$ lines are distinct because the slopes $r$ are distinct.\n\n**Verification of Step 3 – Critical error:**  \nThe argument hinges on the claim  \n\n> “*the condition $a+b\\le n+1$ forces $b\\le n+1-a\\le n+1-t\\le t$.*”\n\nThis inequality is **incorrect**.  Since $a\\ge t+1$, we have  \n\n\\[\nb\\le n+1-a\\le n+1-(t+1)=n-t=k,\n\\]\n\nnot $t$.  In general $k=n-t$ can be **larger** than $t$ (e.g. for $n=7$, $t=2$ we have $k=5>t$).  Therefore the bound $b\\le t$ does **not** hold; many points with $a\\ge t+1$ satisfy $b>t$ and consequently lie **outside** $\\mathcal C_t$.\n\nBecause the vertical lines cover only columns $a\\le t$, they cannot cover points in columns $a>t$ regardless of the value of $b$.  The sunny line $S_r$ associated to column $a$ passes through the single point $(a,1)$ and, as argued, cannot pass through any other point of that column without acquiring slope $0$ or $-1$.  Hence points such as $(a,2)$ (when $2\\le b\\le k$) remain uncovered.  The covering claim “*every point of* $\\mathcal P_n$ *is on at least one of the* $n$ *lines*” is therefore false.\n\nThus the construction for intermediate values $1\\le k\\le n-1$ fails; the proof contains a **critical logical error**.\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Conclusion drawn from the faulty constructions