taining exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n   *Step 1 (non‑sunny part).*  \n   Take the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   V_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n   \\tag{12}\n   \\]\n\n   They are non‑sunny and together cover every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) whose first coordinate does not exceed \\(n-k\\).\n\n   *Step 2 (sunny part).*  \n   For each \\(j=1,\\dots ,k\\) consider the point  \n\n   \\[\n   P_j=\\bigl(n-k+j,\\;k+1-j\\bigr)\\in\\mathcal P_n .\n   \\tag{13}\n   \\]\n\n   The points \\(P_1,\\dots ,P_k\\) lie on the hypotenuse \\(x+y=n+1\\) and belong to distinct columns.  \n   Choose \\(k\\) pairwise different slopes  \n\n   \\[\n   m_j\\in\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,\\infty,-1\\},\\qquad m_j\\neq m_{j'}\\;(j\\neq j').\n   \\tag{14}\n   \\]\n\n   Define the sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   S_j:\\; y-m_j x = (k+1-j)-m_j\\,(n-k+j) .\n   \\tag{15}\n   \\]\n\n   By construction each \\(S_j\\) passes through the corresponding point \\(P_j\\) and, because the slopes are distinct, the lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\) are distinct and sunny.\n\n   *Step 3 (verification of covering).*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\).  \n\n   - If \\(a\\le n-k\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\).  \n\n   - If \\(a>n-k\\) write \\(a=n-k+j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le k\\).  \n     The condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) gives  \n\n     \\[\n     b\\le n+1-a = k+1-j .\n     \\tag{16}\n     \\]\n\n     Hence \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the column of the point \\(P_j\\) and its second coordinate does not exceed that of \\(P_j\\).  \n     The line \\(S_j\\) has slope \\(m_j\\neq -1\\); therefore it meets the vertical line \\(x=a\\) in exactly one point, namely \\((a,b_j)\\) where  \n\n     \\[\n     b_j = m_j a +\\bigl((k+1-j)-m_j\\,(n-k+j)\\bigr)=k+1-j .\n     \\tag{17}\n     \\]\n\n     Consequently \\((a,b_j)=P_j\\) lies on \\(S_j\\).  Since \\(b\\le b_j\\) and the points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) in a fixed column have strictly decreasing second coordinate as we move upward, the only point of that column belonging to \\(S_j\\) is \\(P_j\\).  Nevertheless \\((a,b)\\) is already covered because it lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\) **if** \\(a\\le n\\).  But \\(a\\) is at most \\(n\\) by definition of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\); thus the vertical line \\(x=a\\) is one of the lines \\(V_i\\) **only when** \\(a\\le n-k\\).  In the present case \\(a>n-k\\), so the vertical line is *not* among the chosen non‑sunny lines.  \n\n     To finish the covering we observe that the inequality (16) forces  \n\n     \\[\n     b\\le k+1-j\\le k .\n     \\tag{18}\n     \\]\n\n     Hence \\(b\\le k\\).  The horizontal line  \n\n     \\[\n     H_b:\\; y=b\n     \\tag{19}\n     \\]\n\n     is a non‑sunny line of type (1).  We have not yet used any horizontal lines, so we may add the \\(k\\) horizontal lines  \n\n     \\[\n     H_1,\\dots ,H_k\n     \\tag{20}\n     \\]\n\n     to the family.  Since \\(k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\le n\\), the total number of lines becomes  \n\n     \\[\n     (n-k) \\;(\\text{vertical})\\;+\\;k\\;(\\text{horizontal})\\;=\\;n .\n     \\]\n\n     All these lines are distinct, exactly \\(k\\) of them (the vertical ones) are sunny, and every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(b\\le k\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(H_b\\).  Together with the vertical lines covering the columns \\(a\\le n-k\\), every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is covered.\n\n   Thus we have produced, for each admissible \\(k\\), a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Combining the impossibility result (no configuration can have \\(k\\ge3\\) sunny lines when \\(n=3\\), and in general \\(k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\)) with the explicit constructions for every \\(k\\) satisfying  \n\n   \\[\n   0\\le k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor ,\n   \\]\n\n   we obtain the complete description of the admissible numbers of sunny lines.\n