sis (based on how many vertical lines are used) shows that any configuration with two or more sunny lines leaves at least one point uncovered; therefore only \\(k=0,1\\) are possible.\n\n4. **Partial upper bound.**  \n   Let a *column* be the set \\(\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\mid a\\text{ fixed}\\}\\).  Column \\(a\\) contains \\(h_a=n+1-a\\) points.  \n   A non\u2011vertical line (horizontal, diagonal or sunny) meets a given column in **at most one** point.  Consequently, if a column is not equipped with a vertical line, the points of that column must be supplied by distinct non\u2011vertical lines.  Hence, if \\(v\\) denotes the number of vertical lines used, we must have  \n   \\[\n   n\\;\\ge\\;v+\\sum_{a\\notin S}h_a,\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n   where \\(S\\) is the set of columns that contain a vertical line.  Since the right\u2013hand side of (5) is minimized when the columns in \\(S\\) are the leftmost ones, we obtain the inequality  \n   \\[\n   n\\;\\ge\\;v+\\frac{n(n+1)}2-\\Bigl(v(n+1)-\\frac{v(v+1)}2\\Bigr).\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n   Rearranging (6) yields  \n   \\[\n   v\\;\\ge\\;\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{2n+1-\\sqrt{8n+1}}2\\Bigr\\rceil .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n   Because the number of sunny lines satisfies \\(k\\le n-v\\), (7) gives the general upper bound  \n   \\[\n   k\\;\\le\\;n-\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{2n+1-\\sqrt{8n+1}}2\\Bigr\\rceil .\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n   For \\(n=3,4\\) the right\u2013hand side of (8) equals\u202f1, which agrees with the exhaustive analysis above.\n\n5. **Partial constructions for larger\u202f\\(k\\).**  \n   For any integer \\(k\\) satisfying  \n   \\[\n   0\\le k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{2n+1-\\sqrt{8n+1}}2\\Bigr\\rfloor ,\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n   one can realise \\(k\\) sunny lines as follows.  \n   * Choose the first \\(n-2k\\) columns and place a vertical line in each of them (these are non\u2011sunny).  \n   * The remaining \\(2k\\) columns are paired as \\((c_j,d_j)\\) with \\(c_j=n-2k+j\\) and \\(d_j=n-k+j\\) \\((j=1,\\dots ,k)\\).  \n   * For each pair introduce a sunny line that passes through the top point \\((c_j,\\,n+1-c_j)\\) of the left column and the bottom point \\((d_j,1)\\) of the right column; the slope of this line is \\(-\\dfrac{c_j-1}{d_j-c_j}\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\), so the line is sunny.  \n   * Finally, for each left column \\(c_j\\) add the horizontal line \\(y=n+1-c_j\\) (non\u2011sunny) and for each right column \\(d_j\\) add the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) (non\u2011sunny).  \n   The total number of lines is \\((n-2k)+k+k=n\\); exactly the \\(k\\) lines introduced in the third step are sunny, and a direct verification shows that every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on at least one of the constructed lines.\n\n   The construction works precisely for the range of \\(k\\) given in (9); for larger \\(k\\) the inequality (8) shows that a covering is impossible.\n\n**Conclusion**  \nThe problem is completely solved for \\(n=3\\) and \\(n=4\\) (the only admissible values are \\(k=0,1\\)).  For general \\(n\\) we have proved the universal bounds (1) and (8) and exhibited an explicit construction that attains every integer \\(k\\) in the interval (9).  Determining whether the interval (9) coincides with the exact set of admissible values for all \\(n\\ge5\\) remains an open question."

