            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \n|\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  \nThe three families of non‑sunny lines are\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathcal V_c&:=\\{(c,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\}\\quad\\text{(vertical)},\\\\\n\\mathcal H_c&:=\\{(a,c)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\}\\quad\\text{(horizontal)},\\\\\n\\mathcal D_c&:=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=c\\}\\quad\\text{(diagonal, slope }-1).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nAssume a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nv:=|\\mathcal L\\cap\\{\\text{vertical lines}\\}|,\\qquad\nh:=|\\mathcal L\\cap\\{\\text{horizontal lines}\\}|,\\qquad\nd:=|\\mathcal L\\cap\\{\\text{diagonal lines}\\}|\n\\]\n\nso that  \n\n\\[\nv+h+d=n-k .\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nA vertical line \\(x=c\\) contains precisely the points of the column \\(\\mathcal V_c\\); the total number of points covered by all vertical lines is\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{c\\in S_V}|\\mathcal V_c|\n= v\\,(n+1)-\\frac{v(v+1)}2 ,\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(S_V\\subset\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is the set of abscissae of the chosen vertical lines.  \nThe same formula holds for the horizontal and diagonal families, because the sets \\(\\mathcal H_c\\) and \\(\\mathcal D_c\\) have the same cardinalities as the columns.\n\nHence the number of points covered by the non‑sunny lines is at most  \n\n\\[\nN_{\\text{ns}}=\nv\\!\\left(n+1-\\frac{v+1}{2}\\right)\n+h\\!\\left(n+1-\\frac{h+1}{2}\\right)\n+d\\!\\left(n+1-\\frac{d+1}{2}\\right).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe remaining points,  \n\n\\[\nR:=|\\mathcal P_n|-N_{\\text{ns}},\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nmust be covered by the \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nA sunny line cannot be parallel to any of the three non‑sunny directions; consequently it meets each of the three families in **at most one** point.  Therefore a sunny line can contain at most three points that are not already covered by the non‑sunny lines, and we obtain\n\n\\[\n3k\\;\\ge\\;R .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nInsert (6) and (5) into (7) and use (3) to eliminate \\(h\\) and \\(d\\).  The right‑hand side of (5) is a concave quadratic function of \\((v,h,d)\\) under the linear constraint \\(v+h+d=n-k\\); it is maximised when the three numbers are as equal as possible, which gives the bound\n\n\\[\nN_{\\text{ns}}\\le (n-k)\\!\\left(n+1-\\frac{n-k+1}{2}\\right).\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nWith \\(|\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) we obtain from (7)–(8)\n\n\\[\n3k\\ge\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2-(n-k)\\!\\left(n+1-\\frac{n-k+1}{2}\\right).\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nA straightforward simplification of (9) yields\n\n\\[\nk^{2}+k-\\frac{8n+1}{4}\\le0 .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nSolving the quadratic inequality (10) for the integer \\(k\\) gives\n\n\\[\nk\\le\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{\\sqrt{8n+1}-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nThus **no configuration can contain more than**  \n\n\\[\nK_{\\max}(n):=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{\\sqrt{8n+1}-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nsunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Construction attaining every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le K_{\\max}(n)\\).  \nSet  \n\n\\[\nd:=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{k}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil ,\\qquad\nv:=n-k-d .\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(k\\le K_{\\max}(n)\\) one checks that \\(v\\ge0\\) and \\(v+d\\le n\\).\n\n**(i) Vertical lines.**  \nDraw the \\(v\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=v .\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nThese lines cover all points of the first \\(v\\) columns of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n**(ii) Diagonal lines.**  \nDraw the \\(d\\) diagonal lines of slope \\(-1\\)\n\n\\[\nx+y=n+1,\\;x+y=n,\\;x+y=n-1,\\dots ,x+y=n+2-d .\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nThese lines cover the points that lie on the last \\(d\\) anti‑diagonals of the triangle.\n\nAfter (i) and (ii) the set of uncovered points is exactly\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal Q:=\\{(a,b)\\mid v+1\\le a\\le n-d,\\;1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\}.\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nA direct count shows that \\(\\mathcal Q\\) is a right‑angled triangular lattice of side length \\(k\\); indeed\n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal Q|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{17}\n\\]\n\n**(iii) Sunny lines of slope \\(1\\).**  \nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y-x = i-1 .\n\\tag{18}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(\\ell_i\\) equals \\(1\\); therefore every \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.  \nInside the triangle \\(\\mathcal Q\\) the difference \\(b-a\\) ranges from \\(0\\) to \\(k-1\\); consequently each point of \\(\\mathcal Q\\) lies on exactly one of the lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\).  The lines \\(\\ell_i\\) are distinct, so we have added precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n**(iv) Total number of lines.**  \nThe total number of lines used is  \n\n\\[\nv+d+k\n= (n-k-d)+d+k = n .\n\\tag{19}\n\\]\n\nThus we have produced a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nBecause the construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le K_{\\max}(n)\\), all those values are attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Final answer\n\nCombining the universal upper bound (11) with the explicit construction of §2, we obtain the complete description:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\n\\text{For a given }n\\ge3\\text{ the admissible numbers of sunny lines are }\nk=0,1,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{\\sqrt{8n+1}-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\;}\n\\]\n\nNo other non‑negative integer \\(k\\) can occur, and each integer in the displayed interval can be realised by a suitable configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
