orrect* (the definitions are clear).  The subsequent claims, however, rely on the flawed analysis above.\n\n---\n\n#### 12.  Vertical lines  \n\n> \u201cDraw the \\(v\\) vertical lines  \n> \\[\n> x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=v .\n> \\tag{14}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n*Correct* \u2013 these lines indeed cover the first \\(v\\) columns of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 13.  Diagonal lines  \n\n> \u201cDraw the \\(d\\) diagonal lines of slope \\(-1\\)  \n> \\[\n> x+y=n+1,\\;x+y=n,\\;x+y=n-1,\\dots ,x+y=n+2-d .\n> \\tag{15}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n*Correct* \u2013 these are the \\(d\\) longest anti\u2011diagonals of the triangle.\n\n---\n\n#### 14.  Claim about the uncovered set \\(\\mathcal Q\\)  \n\n> \u201cAfter (i) and (ii) the set of uncovered points is exactly  \n> \\[\n> \\mathcal Q:=\\{(a,b)\\mid v+1\\le a\\le n-d,\\;1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\}.\n> \\tag{16}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n*Issue \u2013 Justification Gap.*  The condition \\(b\\le n+1-a\\) still allows points that lie on the diagonal lines drawn in step (ii).  The true uncovered region must satisfy \\(b\\le n+1-d-a\\).  The definition (16) therefore does **not** describe the set of points that remain uncovered.\n\n---\n\n#### 15.  Claim about the size of \\(\\mathcal Q\\)  \n\n> \u201cA direct count shows that \\(\\mathcal Q\\) is a right\u2011angled triangular lattice of side length \\(k\\); indeed  \n> \\[\n> |\\mathcal Q|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n> \\tag{17}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n*Issue \u2013 Justification Gap.*  The count \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\) is correct for the intended triangle, but because the definition of \\(\\mathcal Q\\) is wrong, the argument does not actually establish the size of the uncovered set.\n\n---\n\n#### 16.  Sunny lines of slope\u202f\\(1\\)  \n\n> \u201cFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) define the line  \n> \\[\n> \\ell_i:\\; y-x = i-1 .\n> \\tag{18}\n> \\]  \n> The slope of \\(\\ell_i\\) equals \\(1\\); therefore every \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.  \n> Inside the triangle \\(\\mathcal Q\\) the difference \\(b-a\\