nd each intersection can be a new uncovered point. The bound “at most three” is false.\n\n* **Location:** Inequality (9) → (10) – “A straightforward simplification of (9) yields\n\\(k^{2}+k-\\frac{8n+1}{4}\\le0\\).”\n  * **Issue:** **Critical Error** – the algebraic simplification is incorrect. Correct manipulation of (9) gives \\(k^{2}+k\\le6k\\), i.e. \\(k\\le5\\), not the displayed quadratic inequality. Hence the derived upper bound is wrong.\n\n* **Location:** Statement (8) – “the right‑hand side of (5) … is maximised when the three numbers are as equal as possible, which gives the bound\n\\(N_{\\text{ns}}\\le (n-k)\\!\\left(n+1-\\frac{n-k+1}{2}\\right).\\)”\n  * **Issue:** **Critical Error** – for a concave quadratic the sum \\(f(v)+f(h)+f(d)\\) under the linear constraint \\(v+h+d=n-k\\) is **maximised** when the variables are as equal as possible, which yields a value **larger** than the expression shown. The bound given is actually a lower bound, not an upper bound.\n\n* **Location:** Definition (16) – “\\(\\displaystyle\\mathcal Q:=\\{(a,b)\\mid v+1\\le a\\le n-d,\\;1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\}.\\)”\n  * **Issue:** **Justification Gap** – this set still contains points that lie on the diagonal lines already drawn, so it is **not** the set of uncovered points. The correct condition should be \\(b\\le n+1-d-a\\).\n\n* **Location:** Claim (17) – “\\(|\\mathcal Q|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\).”\n  * **Issue:** **Justification Gap** – the count is correct for the intended triangular region, but because the definition of \\(\\mathcal Q\\) is wrong, the justification is incomplete.\n\n* **Location:** Construction of sunny lines (18) – “\\(\\ell_i:\\; y-x = i-1\\) for \\(1\\le i\\le k\\).”\n  * **Issue:** **Critical Error** – these lines do **not** cover all points of \\(\\mathcal Q\\) in general (e.g. for \\(n=6,\\;k=3\\) the points \\((3,1)\\) and \\((4,1)\\) are uncovered). The construction fails to achieve the claimed coverage.\n\n* **Location:** Final claim in §2 – “Because the