tion of the non‑sunny lines is legitimate.\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Counting points covered by the vertical lines  \n\n> “A vertical line \\(x=c\\) contains precisely the points of the column \\(\\mathcal V_c\\); the total number of points covered by all vertical lines is\n> \\[\n> \\sum_{c\\in S_V}|\\mathcal V_c|\n> = v\\,(n+1)-\\frac{v(v+1)}2 ,\n> \\tag{4}\n> \\]\n> where \\(S_V\\subset\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is the set of abscissae of the chosen vertical lines.”\n\n*Issue – Justification Gap.*  For a given set \\(S_V\\) we have \\(|\\mathcal V_c|=n+1-c\\), so  \n\\(\\displaystyle\\sum_{c\\in S_V}|\\mathcal V_c|=v(n+1)-\\sum_{c\\in S_V}c\\).  \nThe sum \\(\\sum_{c\\in S_V}c\\) is **minimal** when \\(S_V=\\{1,2,\\dots ,v\\}\\), giving the **maximum** possible number of covered points.  Hence (4) is an **upper bound**, not an exact count, and the solution does not state this distinction.\n\n---\n\n#### 5.  Claim that the same formula holds for horizontal and diagonal families  \n\n> “The same formula holds for the horizontal and diagonal families, because the sets \\(\\mathcal H_c\\) and \\(\\mathcal D_c\\) have the same cardinalities as the columns.”\n\n*Issue – Justification Gap.*  \n* Horizontal lines indeed have \\(|\\mathcal H_c|=n+1-c\\), so the same bound works.  \n* Diagonal lines have \\(|\\mathcal D_c|=c-1\\) (for \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\)), **not** \\(n+1-c\\).  The argument that the cardinalities coincide is false; to obtain a bound one must argue that the longest diagonals are those with the largest \\(c\\), which yields a different expression.  The solution provides no such justification.\n\n---\n\n#### 6.  Definition of \\(N_{\\text{ns}}\\)  \n\n> “Hence the number of points covered by the non‑sunny lines is at most  \n> \\[\n> N_{\\text{ns}}=\n> v\\!\\left(n+1-\\frac{v+1}{2}\\right)\n> +h\\!\\left(n+1-\\frac{h+1}{2}\\right)\n> +d\\!\\left(n+1-\\frac{d+1}{2}\\right).\n> \\tag{5}\n> \\]”\n\n*Issue – Justification Gap.*  The expression adds the contributions of the 